<http://data.silknow.org/object/2d62ac28-552e-3914-bbcb-ce426f32e306>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Linen sampler by Lucke Boten, embroidered with silk and linen; German; dated 1618"@en , "Embroidered sampler"@en , "In their earliest form, samplers were put together as personal reference works for embroiderers. They were trials of patterns and stitches that had been copied from others, and records of particular effects achieved that could be recreated again. This sampler includes a range of repeating patterns, suitable for the decoration of linen. It is the earliest dated German sampler so far known."@en , "There are pieces in similar style in two German museums :\nKunst und Gewerbemuseum, Hamburg, inv.1892.201, dated 1627 and with monogram BB; \nStickmustermuseum Celle, dated 1620 and signed Keneke Bobers.\nAnother piece formerly in the Focke Museum fur bremische Altertumer, Bremen, inv.K1230a, dated 1620 and signed Keneke Hollen, is now lost."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/ff300ff6-6653-37c8-9f0a-6b978b25c257> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/25186c8c-2263-32a0-945d-61195b8214e7> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/cfed3993-db5d-3e46-8736-96c0acee6486> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/e71bab78-18ef-3282-a861-88ba1d4966ed> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Linen sampler by Lucke Boten, embroidered with silk and linen; German; dated 1618"@en , "There are pieces in similar style in two German museums :\nKunst und Gewerbemuseum, Hamburg, inv.1892.201, dated 1627 and with monogram BB; \nStickmustermuseum Celle, dated 1620 and signed Keneke Bobers.\nAnother piece formerly in the Focke Museum fur bremische Altertumer, Bremen, inv.K1230a, dated 1620 and signed Keneke Hollen, is now lost."@en , "Embroidered sampler"@en , "In their earliest form, samplers were put together as personal reference works for embroiderers. They were trials of patterns and stitches that had been copied from others, and records of particular effects achieved that could be recreated again. This sampler includes a range of repeating patterns, suitable for the decoration of linen. It is the earliest dated German sampler so far known."@en ;
        ecrm:P43_has_dimension         <http://data.silknow.org/object/2d62ac28-552e-3914-bbcb-ce426f32e306/dimension/1> , <http://data.silknow.org/object/2d62ac28-552e-3914-bbcb-ce426f32e306/dimension/2> ;
